[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The piece joining the two “parts” is an OSPF virtual link between ABRs r4 and r8 across area 1. Both ends explicitly configure it (r4: “area 1 virtual-link 18.1.4.1”; r8: “area 1 virtual-link 14.1.4.1”), and both routers report it up: r4 shows neighbor 18.1.4.1 Full on interface VLINK0, and r8 shows neighbor 14.1.4.1 Full on its VLINK0. r4’s OSPF area 1 even reports “Number of full virtual adjacencies … 1,” confirming the virtual link is established. End-to-end reachability is normal now (pc0 ↔ pc12 0% loss).

Mechanism: When this virtual link is down, r8 loses backbone (area 0) adjacency and the networks “behind” it (area 2 and connected prefixes) disappear from the rest of the OSPF domain—exactly what monitoring would show as a “far part” vanishing. After a device restart, the virtual link can be temporarily down until OSPF forms adjacencies across the transit area.

Localization: OSPF on r4 (area 0 and 1) and r8 (areas 0, 1, and 2), specifically the virtual-link interface VLINK0 on both.

Root cause: A transient OSPF virtual-link flap after a box restart caused a temporary loss of backbone connectivity across area 1.

Corrective fix: No active fault remains. To prevent recurrences, ensure FRR/ospfd is enabled to start on boot with stable router-ids, and monitor “show ip ospf neighbor” on r4/r8 for VLINK0. For higher resilience, consider replacing the virtual link with a direct area 0 adjacency if topology allows. === END ===